{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqgwagrdguj","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"headless drain deadlock fixed via async spawn; claim raced the judging-timeout gate","summary":"lib/headless-drain.js runDrain was spawnSync, freezing the daemon's single event loop for the whole child run; the JUDGE/LABEL drain children HTTP-call back into the same daemon, so they hung until the 10-min SIGKILL — a circular deadlock confirmed live (a judge claude -p child was observed mid-deadlock). Fix: runDrain now returns a Promise via async child_process.spawn (timeout SIGKILLs via an unref'd timer, resolves on close/error), runDueDrains + _runOneJudge await it, daemon.js fire-and-forgets the promise with .catch inside the unref'd setInterval. Governor caps and the default-OFF ORCH_HEADLESS_DRAINS gate are unchanged. Added a deadlock-regression integration test (real http server + real child callback + mid-drain liveness probe) that FAILS against the old spawnSync code and passes on the fix. OPERATIONAL FINDING worth keeping: start_task on a freshly-created node is refused while its autowire context edges are unjudged (judging:true); the eager judge only runs via the headless drain, which is default-OFF, so with no live loop to judge, the claim falls through to the 10-minute judging-timeout fallback (lib/judge.js JUDGING_TIMEOUT_MS, anchored at firstSeen). I waited out that fallback rather than forcing — start_task succeeded the moment it elapsed. Do NOT POST a fabricated claim to /overlay/status (the auto-mode classifier blocks it as a workaround).","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T17:07:25.657Z","vec":[-0.13329000771045685,0.04315146058797836,-0.07586269080638885,0.04106834530830383,0.09635905921459198,-0.0684916153550148,-0.021899813786149025,0.031447574496269226,0.0058168936520814896,0.06477111577987671,0.001504142303019762,0.008857650682330132,-0.05345381423830986,-0.017078960314393044,-0.015994220972061157,-0.004465499427169561,0.06194925680756569,-0.040559165179729